 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={mod.id} data-module-revision={mod.revision}>
  <h2>{mod.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {mod.revision} · How a fixed-wing airplane flies: the four forces, lift and angle of attack, drag, control and stability, turns and load factor, stalls, and energy. Aircraft-agnostic; worked numbers use an invented example airplane.</p>
  <p>Every figure is interactive: move a control and the drawing and readout follow. Propellers, high-speed (transonic) flight, spins and weight and balance are outside this revision.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>

 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>Everything else in this manual, from a carrier approach to a fight, rests on a handful of ideas: what makes lift, what it costs in drag, what a turn asks of the wing, why a wing stalls, and how altitude and airspeed are two forms of the same energy.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: name the four forces and say how they balance in level flight and in a climb; define angle of attack and explain why a wing stalls at its critical angle at any airspeed; use the lift equation to explain why a slower airplane needs more angle of attack; describe parasite and induced drag and where total drag is least; name the three axes and the control for each; tell static from dynamic stability; work out the load factor and stall speed in a level turn; read a stall boundary and maneuvering speed; and explain why the throttle controls total energy and the elevator divides it between altitude and airspeed.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a conceptual reference, not a flight manual. The example airplane (2,300 lb, 174 ft² of wing, CL<sub>max</sub> 1.6) is invented to put numbers on the equations. Your airplane's speeds and limits come from its own flight manual.</p>

 <h2 id="forces">The four forces</h2>
 <p>Four forces act on an airplane in flight. <strong>Thrust</strong> is the forward force produced by the powerplant. <strong>Drag</strong> is the rearward, retarding force caused by the disruption of airflow by the wing, fuselage and other protruding objects. <strong>Lift</strong> is produced by the dynamic effect of the air acting on the wing. <strong>Weight</strong> is the combined load of the airplane, crew, fuel and cargo or baggage, pulled downward by gravity. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-1–5-2')}</a></p>
 <p>In steady flight the sum of these opposing forces is always zero. It is tempting to say lift equals weight and thrust equals drag, but the handbook cautions that this can be misleading: in a climb, part of the weight acts along the flight path, in the same direction as drag, and thrust has to overcome it too. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-2, 5-23–5-24')}</a> Newton's laws are the frame: a force is needed to change motion, force equals mass times acceleration, and every action has an equal and opposite reaction. <a href="#source-phak">{k('4-5–4-6')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="forces" aria-labelledby="forces-title">
  <h3 id="forces-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — Forces in steady flight (side view)</h3>
  <p>Choose an airspeed and a flight-path angle. The drawing resolves weight along and across the path and shows the thrust needed to hold the speed.</p>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="forces-speed">Airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="forces-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="55" max="140" step="1" value="90" /></label>
   <label for="forces-gamma">Flight-path angle <output for="gamma"></output><input id="forces-gamma" name="gamma" type="range" min="-10" max="10" step="1" value="0" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 420" role="img" aria-label="Side view of the airplane with lift, weight, thrust and drag vectors"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Level at 90 kt the example airplane has 212 lb of drag, so thrust is 212 lb and lift equals the 2,300 lb weight. Climbing at 5° at the same speed, 200 lb of the weight acts rearward along the path: thrust rises to 412 lb and lift falls slightly, to 2,291 lb.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Steady straight flight: along the path, thrust = drag + W sin γ; across it, lift = W cos γ, with γ the flight-path angle. Drag is the example airplane's level-flight drag at that speed (Figure 4), used for the climb too. Sea level, still air. Thrust is assumed to act along the flight path.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="airfoil">The wing and angle of attack</h2>
 <p>A wing's cross-section is an <strong>airfoil</strong>. Its rounded front is the <strong>leading edge</strong>, its narrow, tapered back the <strong>trailing edge</strong>, and the straight line joining them is the <strong>chord line</strong>. The upper surface is usually more curved than the lower; that curvature is <strong>camber</strong>, traced by the <strong>mean camber line</strong>, equidistant from the two surfaces. <a href="#source-phak">{k('4-6–4-7')}</a></p>
 <p>The <strong>angle of attack</strong> (AOA) is the acute angle between the chord line and the direction of the relative wind. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-2–5-3')}</a> Inclined to the airflow, the wing makes the air over the upper surface move faster than the air beneath; by Bernoulli's principle, faster-moving air has lower pressure, so the pressure above falls below atmospheric. The downward and backward flow off the wing, the <strong>downwash</strong>, is pushed down; by Newton's third law, the wing is pushed up and forward. <a href="#source-phak">{k('4-6–4-8')}</a> The handbook warns that it is neither accurate nor useful to assign fixed shares of lift to the upper and lower surfaces: they vary with conditions and design. <a href="#source-phak">{k('4-7')}</a></p>
 <p>More angle of attack gives more lift, up to a maximum. Past that <strong>critical angle of attack</strong>, the flow separates from the upper surface, lift falls away rapidly and the wing <strong>stalls</strong>. The peak of the lift curve is CL<sub>max</sub>. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-3, 5-25')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="airfoil" aria-labelledby="airfoil-title">
  <h3 id="airfoil-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — Airfoil, relative wind and the lift curve</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="airfoil-alpha">Angle of attack <output for="alpha"></output><input id="airfoil-alpha" name="alpha" type="range" min="-4" max="24" step="1" value="6" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-label="Airfoil cross-section at the chosen angle of attack with streamlines, lift vector and an inset lift curve"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 6° angle of attack the illustrative section has a lift coefficient of 0.80. Lift rises steadily to CLmax 1.6 at the 16° critical angle; beyond it the flow separates and lift falls.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">The section is a NACA 2412 shape (2% camber, 12% thick) drawn for its looks. The lift curve is illustrative: zero lift at −2°, 0.1 per degree, rounding to CL<sub>max</sub> 1.6 at a 16° critical angle; the post-stall fall-off and the streamlines are sketches, not measurements. Real wings have their own curve.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="lift">The lift equation</h2>
 <p>Lift is L = ½ ρ V² S C<sub>L</sub>: air density ρ, true airspeed V, wing area S and the lift coefficient C<sub>L</sub>, which the pilot sets through angle of attack. Lift is proportional to the square of the airspeed. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-4')}</a> A given airplane always stalls at the same angle of attack, regardless of airspeed, weight, load factor or density altitude; what changes is the <em>speed</em> at which it reaches that angle. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-25')}</a></p>
 <p>In level flight lift must carry the weight, so as speed falls the wing needs more C<sub>L</sub>, which means more angle of attack. At some speed even CL<sub>max</sub> is not enough: that is the 1 G stall speed.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="lift-speed" aria-labelledby="lift-title">
  <h3 id="lift-title" class="fig-title">Figure 3 — Lift coefficient needed for level flight</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="lift-speed">Airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="lift-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="40" max="160" step="1" value="70" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 3, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-label="Chart of the lift coefficient needed against airspeed, with the CLmax line and the stall speed"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 70 kt the example airplane needs a lift coefficient of 0.80, about 6° on the illustrative lift curve. Its 1 G stall speed, where CLmax 1.6 just carries the weight, is 49 kt.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">C<sub>L</sub> = 2W ÷ (ρ V² S) for the example airplane at sea level, ρ = 1.225 kg/m³; 1 G stall speed V<sub>S</sub> = √(2W ÷ (ρ S CL<sub>max</sub>)) = 49.4 kt true airspeed. Angle of attack read from Figure 2's illustrative curve.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="drag">Drag</h2>
 <p><strong>Parasite drag</strong> is made up of all the forces that work to slow an airplane's movement: <strong>form drag</strong> from its shape and the airflow around it, <strong>interference drag</strong> where airstreams meet and create eddies and turbulence, and <strong>skin friction drag</strong> from air moving over the surface. Parasite drag increases as the square of the airspeed. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-6–5-8')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Induced drag</strong> is inherent whenever a wing is producing lift. It varies inversely with the square of the airspeed, so it is greatest when slow, at high angle of attack. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-7–5-8')}</a></p>
 <p>Add the two and total drag has a minimum. Flown steadily at that point, the lift-to-drag ratio is at its maximum, L/D<sub>max</sub>, and total drag is least. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-5–5-6')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="drag" aria-labelledby="drag-title">
  <h3 id="drag-title" class="fig-title">Figure 4 — Parasite, induced and total drag</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="drag-speed">Airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="drag-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="40" max="160" step="1" value="90" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 4, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-label="Drag curves against airspeed with the minimum-drag point marked"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 90 kt the example airplane has 148 lb of parasite and 64 lb of induced drag, 212 lb in all. Total drag is least, 194 lb, at 73 kt, where the two are equal and L/D is 11.9.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Level flight, sea level. Parasite D<sub>p</sub> = q S C<sub>D0</sub>, C<sub>D0</sub> = 0.031; induced D<sub>i</sub> = k W² ÷ (q S), k = 1 ÷ (π e AR) with aspect ratio 7.4 and efficiency e = 0.75; q = ½ ρ V². The minimum is where D<sub>p</sub> = D<sub>i</sub>, giving L/D<sub>max</sub> = 1 ÷ (2√(k C<sub>D0</sub>)). Example values, not any type.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="ground-effect">Wingtip vortices and ground effect</h2>
 <p>At the wingtip the higher pressure beneath the wing flows around to the lower pressure above, forming a tip vortex and a downwash that reduce lift on that part of the wing. <a href="#source-phak">{k('4-9')}</a> Close to the ground, the surface interferes with these airflow patterns. Ground effect is most noticeable below a height of one wingspan; it reduces induced drag by about 1.4% at one span, 23.5% at a quarter span and 47.6% at a tenth of a span. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-11–5-12')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="axes">Axes and flight controls</h2>
 <p>An airplane rotates about three imaginary axes that pass through its centre of gravity (CG). Motion about the <strong>longitudinal axis</strong> (nose to tail) is <strong>roll</strong>; about the <strong>lateral axis</strong> (parallel to a line from wingtip to wingtip) is <strong>pitch</strong>; about the <strong>vertical axis</strong> is <strong>yaw</strong>. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-12–5-13')}</a></p>
 <p>The ailerons, elevator (or stabilator) and rudder are the primary flight controls. <a href="#source-phak">{k('6-2')}</a> The <strong>ailerons</strong> control roll: they move in opposite directions, so turning the wheel right raises the right aileron and lowers the left, increasing lift on the left wing and decreasing it on the right, and the airplane rolls right. The <strong>elevator</strong> controls pitch about the lateral axis, and the <strong>rudder</strong> controls yaw about the vertical axis. <a href="#source-phak">{k('6-3, 6-5, 6-8')}</a></p>
 <p>A roll brings a side effect, <strong>adverse yaw</strong>: the nose yaws opposite to the direction of bank, because of the differential drag and the slightly different speeds of the two wings. <a href="#source-phak">{k('6-3')}</a> Coordinated rudder counters it; in coordinated flight the ball of the slip/skid indicator is centred. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-8')}</a> Trim relieves the pilot of holding constant control pressure, and flaps increase both lift and induced drag at a given angle of attack. <a href="#source-phak">{k('6-8, 6-10')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="axes" aria-labelledby="axes-title">
  <h3 id="axes-title" class="fig-title">Figure 5 — The three axes and the primary flight controls</h3>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Axis</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="axis" value="roll" checked /> Roll</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="axis" value="pitch" /> Pitch</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="axis" value="yaw" /> Yaw</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="axes-input">Control input <output for="input"></output><input id="axes-input" name="input" type="range" min="-100" max="100" step="5" value="40" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 5, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 400" role="img" aria-label="Three-dimensional line drawing of an airplane rotating about the selected axis, with the controlling surface deflected"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Roll is about the longitudinal axis and is controlled by the ailerons; pitch is about the lateral axis, controlled by the elevator; yaw is about the vertical axis, controlled by the rudder. All three axes pass through the centre of gravity.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Original line drawing of a generic light airplane. Surface travel (up to 25°) and attitude change (up to 30°) are illustrative and proportional to the input; a real airplane's response depends on speed and keeps changing while a control is held.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="stability">Stability</h2>
 <p><strong>Static stability</strong> is the initial tendency after a disturbance. With <em>positive</em> static stability the airplane initially tends to return to its original state; with <em>neutral</em>, it tends to stay in the new condition; with <em>negative</em>, it tends to continue away. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-14–5-15')}</a> <strong>Dynamic stability</strong> is the response over time: whether the motion, once started back, damps out, keeps going, or grows. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-14–5-15')}</a></p>
 <p>In pitch, with the CG forward of the wing's centre of lift and an aerodynamic tail-down force from the horizontal tail, the airplane usually tries to return to a safe flying attitude. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-15–5-17')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="stability" aria-labelledby="stab-title">
  <h3 id="stab-title" class="fig-title">Figure 6 — Static and dynamic stability after a disturbance</h3>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Response</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="static-positive" /> Static positive</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="static-neutral" /> Static neutral</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="static-negative" /> Static negative</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="dynamic-positive" checked /> Dynamic positive</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="dynamic-neutral" /> Dynamic neutral</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="kind" value="dynamic-negative" /> Dynamic negative</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 6, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 320" role="img" aria-label="Displacement against time for the selected kind of stability, with the others of its group shown faintly"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Positive dynamic stability: the airplane overshoots trim but each swing is smaller and it settles. Neutral: the swings stay the same size. Negative: they grow, even though each swing starts back toward trim.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Illustrative curves only: exponential returns and departures, and 4-second oscillations with damping, unchanged amplitude or growth. They show the shape of each kind of response, not any airplane's numbers.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="turns">Turns and load factor</h2>
 <p>Banking tilts lift with the wing. Its <strong>horizontal component</strong> is the force that pulls the airplane from a straight path into a turn; its <strong>vertical component</strong> is now less than the weight, so to hold altitude the pilot increases angle of attack, with back pressure on the elevator, until the vertical component again equals the weight. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-22–5-23')}</a></p>
 <p>The ratio of lift to weight is the <strong>load factor</strong>, n, felt as G. In a level, coordinated turn n = 1 ÷ cos(bank): a level 60° banked turn is 2 G, and the stall speed is then about 41% higher than the 1 G stall speed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-9')}</a> Stall speed is higher in a level turn than in straight-and-level flight <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-25')}</a>; it rises with the square root of the load factor, which is where the 41% comes from (√2 ≈ 1.41).</p>
 <p>At a given airspeed, the rate of turn depends on the horizontal component of lift, so more bank turns faster; and an increase in airspeed at the same bank increases the turn radius. <a href="#source-phak">{k('5-22–5-24')}</a> In numbers: radius r = V² ÷ (g tan φ) and rate ω = g tan φ ÷ V, so at a fixed bank doubling the speed quadruples the radius and halves the rate.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Load factor and stall speed by bank angle" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Level, coordinated turn: load factor n = 1 ÷ cos φ; stall speed × √n</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Bank</th><th scope="col">Load factor</th><th scope="col">Stall speed increase</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">15°</th><td>1.04 G</td><td>2%</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">30°</th><td>1.15 G</td><td>7%</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">45°</th><td>1.41 G</td><td>19%</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">60°</th><td>2.00 G</td><td>41%</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">70°</th><td>2.92 G</td><td>71%</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">80°</th><td>5.76 G</td><td>140%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="turn" aria-labelledby="turn-title">
  <h3 id="turn-title" class="fig-title">Figure 7 — Bank, load factor and the turn circle</h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="turn-bank">Bank angle <output for="bank"></output><input id="turn-bank" name="bank" type="range" min="0" max="80" step="1" value="30" /></label>
   <label for="turn-speed">Airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="turn-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="50" max="300" step="5" value="100" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 7, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 400" role="img" aria-label="Rear view of the banked airplane with lift resolved into vertical and horizontal parts, and a plan view of its turn circle"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. 30° of bank needs 1.15 G. At 100 kt the turn radius is 1,534 ft and the rate 6.3° per second, a full circle in 57 s. The example airplane's stall speed rises from 49 to 53 kt.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Level, coordinated turn at constant true airspeed: n = 1 ÷ cos φ; r = V² ÷ (g tan φ); ω = g tan φ ÷ V, with g = 9.80665 m/s². Stall speed is the example airplane's 49.4 kt × √n. The plan view scales to fit.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="stalls">Stalls</h2>
 <p>A stall happens when the wing exceeds its critical angle of attack, not when the airplane is "too slow". It is possible to exceed the critical angle of attack at any airspeed, at any attitude and at any power setting. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-9, 5-12')}</a> The most important action for an impending or full stall is to <strong>reduce the angle of attack</strong>; common errors include trying to level the wings or add power before doing so. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-15, 5-21')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Accelerated stall</strong>: any stall entered at more than 1 G, such as in a steep turn; it is trained at about 45° of bank, at or below maneuvering speed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-19')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Cross-control stall</strong>: the critical angle of attack is exceeded with aileron applied one way and rudder the other, often in a skidding base-to-final turn, and it can lead to a spin. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-20')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Spin</strong>: an aggravated stall in which at least one wing is stalled and the airplane autorotates. Its phases are entry, incipient, developed and recovery. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-22–5-24')}</a> Spin recovery follows the airplane's flight manual.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The FAA treats as an <strong>upset</strong> an event that unintentionally exceeds normal flight parameters: pitch more than 25° nose up, more than 10° nose down, bank more than 45°, or an inappropriate airspeed within those limits. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-1')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="envelope">The stall boundary and maneuvering speed</h2>
 <p>Put the turn and the lift equation together: at any speed, CL<sub>max</sub> limits how much load factor the wing can make, n<sub>max</sub> = (V ÷ V<sub>S</sub>)². Plotted against airspeed, that is the stall boundary of a V-n (V-g) diagram. The structure sets a second limit, the limit load factor. Where they meet is the <strong>maneuvering speed</strong>, V<sub>A</sub>: the maximum speed at which the positive design load limit can be imposed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-19')}</a> It follows from the stall boundary that below V<sub>A</sub> a full pull stalls the wing before it can reach the limit load, while above V<sub>A</sub> it can exceed it first.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="envelope" aria-labelledby="env-title">
  <h3 id="env-title" class="fig-title">Figure 8 — Stall boundary, limit load and V<sub>A</sub></h3>
  <div class="controls row">
   <label for="env-speed">Airspeed <output for="speed"></output><input id="env-speed" name="speed" type="range" min="30" max="180" step="1" value="80" /></label>
   <label for="env-g">Load factor demanded <output for="g"></output><input id="env-g" name="g" type="range" min="1" max="8" step="0.1" value="2" /></label>
   <label for="env-limit">Limit load factor <output for="limit"></output><input id="env-limit" name="limit" type="range" min="2.5" max="7.5" step="0.1" value="3.8" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 8, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 660 380" role="img" aria-label="Load factor against airspeed with the stall boundary, the limit load and maneuvering speed"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. At 80 kt the example airplane's wing can make at most 2.6 G before it stalls, so 2 G is inside the envelope. With a limit load factor of 3.8, the stall boundary meets the limit at a maneuvering speed of 96 kt.</p>
  <div class="feedback derived" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Positive-G side only, example airplane at maximum weight and sea level: n<sub>max</sub> = (V ÷ 49.4 kt)², V<sub>A</sub> = V<sub>S</sub> √n<sub>limit</sub>. The limit load factor is adjustable and set to 3.8 as an example; the real limits, the negative side and the high-speed end of the diagram come from the airplane's flight manual. V<sub>A</sub> falls at lighter weights, because V<sub>S</sub> does.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="energy">Energy: altitude and airspeed</h2>
 <p>An airplane's total mechanical energy is the sum of its potential energy, in altitude (mgh), and its kinetic energy, in airspeed (½mV²). <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-1, 4-18')}</a> With no change in thrust or drag, altitude and airspeed change in opposite directions when energy is exchanged: zoom up and the airplane slows; dive and it speeds up. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Dividing by weight gives <strong>energy height</strong>, E<sub>S</sub> = h + V² ÷ 2g, an altitude that measures both. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-7')}</a> The handbook's example: an airplane cruising at 100 kt and 6,000 ft has about the same energy height, 6,500 ft, as one at 240 kt and 4,000 ft, and either could ideally zoom to 6,500 ft. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-8')}</a> Specific excess power, P<sub>S</sub> = (T − D)V ÷ W, is the rate at which energy height can be gained. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-3')}</a></p>
 <p>The controls split the job: <strong>the throttle is the total energy controller; the elevator is the energy distribution controller</strong>, between altitude and airspeed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-4')}</a> At low speed and high angle of attack, on the back side of the power curve, there is little or no excess power. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-5')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="energy" aria-labelledby="energy-title">
