 </section>

 <h2 id="terms">Terms</h2>
 <dl class="jargon">
  <div><dt>Angle of attack (AOA)</dt><dd>The acute angle between the chord line and the relative wind.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Critical angle of attack</dt><dd>The angle of maximum lift (CL<sub>max</sub>); beyond it the wing stalls.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Chord line</dt><dd>A straight line joining the leading and trailing edges of an airfoil.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Camber</dt><dd>The curvature of an airfoil, traced by the mean camber line.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Parasite drag</dt><dd>Form, interference and skin friction drag; grows with the square of airspeed.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Induced drag</dt><dd>Drag that comes with making lift; varies inversely with the square of airspeed.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>L/D<sub>max</sub></dt><dd>The best lift-to-drag ratio, where total drag is least.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Load factor</dt><dd>Lift divided by weight, felt as G; 1 ÷ cos(bank) in a level, coordinated turn.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Maneuvering speed (V<sub>A</sub>)</dt><dd>The maximum speed at which the positive design load limit can be imposed.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Adverse yaw</dt><dd>Yaw opposite to the direction of bank when rolling, from differential aileron drag.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Energy height (E<sub>S</sub>)</dt><dd>h + V² ÷ 2g: altitude plus the altitude the airspeed could ideally buy.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Ground effect</dt><dd>The reduction in induced drag when flying within about a wingspan of the surface.</dd></div>
 </dl>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>Can a wing stall at high airspeed?</summary><p>Yes. A stall is exceeding the critical angle of attack, which can happen at any airspeed, attitude or power setting: a hard pull at high speed is an accelerated stall.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The airplane is about to stall. What is the first thing to do?</summary><p>Reduce the angle of attack. Levelling the wings or adding power first are common errors.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You halve your airspeed in level flight. What happens to the lift coefficient the wing needs?</summary><p>It quadruples, because lift goes with the square of airspeed. The wing gets it from a higher angle of attack, until CL<sub>max</sub> runs out.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You slow down below L/D<sub>max</sub> speed. Does drag go down?</summary><p>No. Below L/D<sub>max</sub>, induced drag dominates and total drag rises as you slow.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What load factor does a level 60° bank need, and what happens to the stall speed?</summary><p>2 G, and the stall speed is about 41% higher than at 1 G (√2 ≈ 1.41).</p></details>
 <details><summary>At the same bank, you fly twice as fast. What happens to the turn?</summary><p>The radius is four times larger and the rate is half.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Which control changes your total energy, and which trades altitude for speed?</summary><p>The throttle changes total energy; the elevator divides it between altitude and airspeed.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You roll right using aileron alone. Which way does the nose yaw?</summary><p>Left, opposite to the bank: adverse yaw. Coordinated right rudder keeps the ball centred.</p></details>

